 ## Run many projects in parallel
 
-A [Cursor Project](https://cursor.com/blog/projects) gives one coordinator agent a persistent thread. The coordinator doesn't write code. It directs subagents, which run in the cloud by default, so the work continues when your laptop is closed. Start your prompts to the coordinator with `/igoroh-mode`, and the subagents it spawns follow the playbooks. Each agent it starts reads [`worker.md`](../../skills/igoroh-mode/worker.md) first, so workers hold to the kickoff's bar, decide reversible calls instead of asking you, and open PRs ready for review.
+Herdr runs several Claude Code sessions at once, one tab per project, and each session's workers sit in the sidebar under it. Workers run on this machine, so a night of work needs the machine awake and Herdr open. Steering is the same in every tab, `hc read` to see what a worker is doing and `hc send` to answer it.
 
 A few habits help:
 
-- Give each body of work its own Project, such as a feature, a migration, a perf push, or a tech-debt cleanup. Several can run side by side.
-- Drag related chats into the Project, finished ones included. They become context for every agent in it.
-- Give each PR a verification swarm before it merges, and let Autopilot-stack or Autopilot-full carry the queue.
+- Give each body of work its own tab, such as a feature, a migration, a perf push, or a tech-debt cleanup. Several can run side by side.
+- Start every task in a tab with `/istack:igoroh-mode`, so the workers it starts follow the playbooks.
+- When a worker needs what an earlier chat knew, put that transcript's path in its brief, `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<session>.jsonl`, and start it with `--claude`.
+- Give each PR a verification swarm before it merges, and let Autopilot-stack or Autopilot-full carry the queue. The swarm starts one worker per slice.
 - Ask the coordinator for a plan backed by data, and have it answer open questions with prototypes before it asks you.
 
